  "problem": "A scientist measured $5$ particular features, ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{5},$ for each specimen among a group of animals in danger of extinction. The collected data was stored in the standardized observation matrix $X,$ where each row contains measurements for a particular specimen.\nThe singular values of $X$ are \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{1}=7,\\,{\\sigma }_{2}=2,\\,{\\sigma }_{3}=1,\\,{\\sigma }_{4}=0.6,\\,{\\sigma }_{5}=0.4.\n$$\n\nThe scientist then converted the original observation matrix $X$ into a new matrix $Y$ using the equation $Y=X{V}_{1},$ where ${V}_{1}$ is the $5\\times 1$ matrix consisting of the first principal component of the data. What percentage of the original variance is preserved in the new transformed matrix $Y$ compared to the initial observation matrix $X?$",
